---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/tree.json';
import Evidence from '@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Evidence.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
/* The five-word ladder is shared across the sibling archives so a level means
   the same thing wherever a reader meets it. Two of this page's marks are NOT
   levels on it and must not pretend to be: 'unknown' is the absence of a claim,
   and 'excluded' is a claim tested and failed. They get their own chips. */
const LADDER = new Set(['documented', 'probable', 'inferred', 'family', 'disputed']);
const OFF = { unknown: 'Not established', excluded: 'Ruled out' };
---
<style is:global>
  /* The two marks that are not evidence levels are drawn deliberately unlike
     the ladder: no fill, a dashed edge, so they cannot be mistaken for a grade. */
  .ev-off { border-style: dashed; background: transparent; }
  .ev-unknown { color: var(--ink-3); }
  .ev-excluded { color: var(--disp); }
</style>
<Base title={data.title} subtitle={data.dek}
  lede="A family tree that shows only names is a claim without a citation. This one grades every person on the same five-word ladder the other archives use, so a level means the same thing wherever a reader meets it — and marks the two things that are not levels at all.">

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>How to read it</h3>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        {data.legend.map((l) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{LADDER.has(l.k)
              ? <Evidence level={l.k} />
              : <span class={'ev ev-off ev-' + l.k}>{l.label}</span>}</td>
            <td class="small muted">{l.what}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>Living people are named and nothing more.</strong> No dates, no ages, nothing that could
      be worked backwards into one.
    </p>
  </div>

  {data.generations.map((g) => (
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>{g.label} <span class="small muted">&mdash; generation {g.n}</span></h3>
      {g.people.map((p) => (
        <div style="margin:0 0 1.15rem 0;">
          <h4 style="margin:0 0 .15rem 0;">
            {p.name}{' '}
            {LADDER.has(p.status)
              ? <Evidence level={p.status} />
              : <span class={'ev ev-off ev-' + p.status}>{OFF[p.status]}</span>}
          </h4>
          {p.dates && <p class="small" style="margin:.1rem 0;"><strong>{p.dates}</strong></p>}
          {p.place && <p class="small muted" style="margin:.1rem 0;">{p.place}</p>}
          <p class="small muted" style="margin:.3rem 0 0 0;">{p.how}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  ))}

  <div class="panel" id="twolines">
    <h3>Why a family the DNA rules out is still on this page</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Kurt Israel Luwinski is not Derrick&rsquo;s biological father.</strong> The DNA excludes
      him twice over &mdash; 0.0% Ashkenazi where a quarter would be required, and a Y haplogroup that
      is North Sea rather than Ashkenazi. That is the central finding of this archive and nothing here
      softens it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It does not follow that Kurt&rsquo;s family is not Derrick&rsquo;s.</strong> He carries
      their name. He has carried it his whole life, it is the name on this archive, and it reached him
      from a man who was married to his mother when he was born and is his father on every document
      that exists. <strong>A tree drawn only from chromosomes would delete all six people in the block
      above</strong> &mdash; and with them the reason this archive was ever built.
    </p>
    <p class="pull">
      Descent is not the only thing a family is. This page grades evidence; it does not grade belonging.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the line is kept, and kept honestly. <strong>It is set apart from the ancestry rather than
      mixed into it</strong>, because to fold Jacob Leibholz in beside Henry Robert Wear as though both
      were grandfathers in the same sense would be a false claim about biology. And it is graded on the
      same ladder as everything else: these people are <em>documented</em> because the documents are
      real, whatever the chromosomes say about the join at the top.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And the family has now answered what the record could not.</strong> This panel said,
      until 28 September 2026, that nothing read so far showed what part Kurt had in
      Derrick&rsquo;s upbringing, and that Derrick was living and could answer what the record cannot.
      He can, and the answer came in four words:
    </p>
    <p class="pull">
      <strong>Kurt raised Derrick.</strong>
      <span class="small">&mdash; David Defranceski, Derrick&rsquo;s son-in-law, 28 September 2026
      <span class="ev ev-family">Family</span></span>
    </p>
    <p>
      So both things are true and the page says both. <strong>Kurt Israel Luwinski is not
      Derrick&rsquo;s biological father &mdash; and he is the man who brought him up.</strong> The
      block above is not a line of names he happened to inherit. It is the family of the house he
      grew up in.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>Why the papers made that look unlikely, and why they do not actually contradict it.</strong>
      Kurt sued Ivone in 1948 and the child was born after he had filed, which is how this archive
      wrote it up &mdash; <em>within months of the wedding and before the child was born</em>. But the
      action was for <strong>restitution of conjugal rights</strong> and was not granted until
      <strong>1951</strong>: in South African practice that was the first required step of a divorce,
      an order for a spouse&rsquo;s return that converted to divorce only on non-compliance. The
      marriage therefore ran on paper for about three years and the child was born inside it.
      <strong>A filing date is not a departure date, and this archive had been treating it as one.</strong>
      A document records an act, not a life.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>And the weight falls where you would not expect.</strong> Of all the dead this archive
      holds, the ones it has read most closely are on this line and not on the blood line &mdash;
      Jacob Leibholz to Auschwitz in February 1943, his brothers Samuel in April and Oskar in October
      1944, Georg and Lydia at Kulmhof in 1942. <strong>They are not Derrick&rsquo;s ancestors and they
      are his family&rsquo;s dead</strong>, and an archive that dropped them for failing a DNA test
      would be measuring the wrong thing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What the shape of this tree says</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>It is lopsided, and the lopsidedness is the finding.</strong> The Wear side now runs back
      five generations to a harness maker in Bermondsey and a farm labourer in Aldenham. The Figueiredo
      side stops dead at Derrick's grandparents, whose given names nobody has ever established.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is not because one family mattered more. <strong>It is because English civil registration
      from 1837 is indexed, digitised and searchable from a desk, and Portuguese colonial registration
      in Louren&ccedil;o Marques is not.</strong> Four generations of Wears were added in a single day
      from a subscription; one generation of Figueiredos has resisted a year of letters.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>Updated 16 September.</strong> The Castle hole closed the same day it was written, and
      not by research &mdash; <strong>by a DNA match sharing his tree.</strong> A second cousin
      through the Castles, in the United Kingdom, sharing 333.9 cM, gave read access to his Ancestry tree and it carried
      <strong>George William Castle&rsquo;s parents, a birthplace at Willian, a marriage in 1882, and
      nine children</strong>. <strong>And the last hole &mdash; Ivone&rsquo;s parents &mdash; has since closed</strong>, by the
      same means. The two documents this paragraph named as the ones that would answer it are both in
      hand: her <strong>1922 birth registration</strong> and her <strong>death certificate</strong>,
      photographed from the family&rsquo;s own papers in September 2026 and supplied by Derrick. They
      give <strong>Joaquim J&uacute;lio de Figueiredo and Le&oacute;nia dos Santos Mota</strong>, all
      four of her grandparents, and the parishes her parents came from.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>Which is its own lesson.</strong> Four generations of Wears came from a paid
      subscription in a day. A generation of Castles came from a stranger answering a message. <em>The
      Figueiredos have resisted both.</em>
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      See also <a href={base + '/names/'}>her names</a>,
      <a href={base + '/dna/'}>the DNA</a>,
      <a href={base + '/open-questions/'}>open questions</a> and
      <a href={base + '/register/'}>the register</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
